WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:10.100
Human genes encode 518 protein kinases, including 58 receptor tyrosine kinases.

2
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:17.500
Baker's yeast has roughly 120 to 130 kinases, and none of those receptors.

3
00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:20.280
So where did ours come from?

4
00:00:21.233 --> 00:00:26.013
You might expect humans to have invented dozens of new signaling pathways.

5
00:00:27.473 --> 00:00:28.593
Genomes show the opposite.

6
00:00:30.073 --> 00:00:34.553
Fruit flies and humans share the same short list of eight core animal pathways.

7
00:00:35.913 --> 00:00:40.133
What changed on the way to vertebrates was how many copies each layer holds.

8
00:00:41.217 --> 00:00:45.337
Compare humans with four invertebrates, adjusting for gene number.

9
00:00:46.717 --> 00:00:51.997
Each ratio is a family's share of human genes, divided by its share of invertebrate

10
00:00:51.997 --> 00:00:55.717
genes. One means growth in step with genes.

11
00:00:56.997 --> 00:01:00.117
All protein kinases score 1.2.

12
00:01:00.117 --> 00:01:02.917
MAP kinases score 1.8.

13
00:01:04.417 --> 00:01:06.377
EGF receptors, 2.5.

14
00:01:06.377 --> 00:01:10.437
Receptor tyrosine kinases, 2.9.

15
00:01:11.467 --> 00:01:13.847
Where did the extra receptors come from?

16
00:01:15.187 --> 00:01:18.987
Copying errors duplicate single genes, or sometimes whole-genomes.

17
00:01:20.367 --> 00:01:23.647
Early jawed vertebrates went through two whole-genome doublings.

18
00:01:24.927 --> 00:01:29.387
One EGF receptor times two makes two, and two times two makes four.

19
00:01:30.547 --> 00:01:35.207
That is the likely route from one receptor, as in flies, to four in humans.

20
00:01:36.367 --> 00:01:41.367
Once a gene is copied, the original keeps working, so the spare is free to change.

21
00:01:42.647 --> 00:01:47.027
Most spares decay into pseudogenes, copies that make no working protein.

22
00:01:48.347 --> 00:01:50.107
Or the copies divide the old job.

23
00:01:51.407 --> 00:01:53.787
Say the old receptor worked in gut and nerve.

24
00:01:54.887 --> 00:01:59.827
One copy loses the DNA that turns it on in gut, the other the nerve DNA.

25
00:02:01.387 --> 00:02:02.567
Now both must stay.

26
00:02:03.617 --> 00:02:09.437
A third, rarer outcome: one copy evolves a new job, like binding a different signal.

27
00:02:10.737 --> 00:02:16.377
One ancestral receptor becomes a family of relatives listening to different outside

28
00:02:16.377 --> 00:02:20.677
messages. But more receptors raise a wiring puzzle.

29
00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:27.240
Suppose one pathway has one receptor over a three-kinase core, four genes in all.

30
00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:32.360
Four separate pathways would take four times four, or sixteen genes.

31
00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:37.940
Four receptors sharing one core take four plus three, just seven genes.

32
00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:42.120
Looking at our genome bars, which layout fits better?

33
00:02:48.083 --> 00:02:53.263
A shared core fits better, because receptors multiplied faster than the kinases

34
00:02:53.263 --> 00:02:57.563
below them. Separate tracks would grow both layers equally.

35
00:02:58.983 --> 00:03:04.043
Receptors funnel into a few heavily connected proteins called hubs, such as Ras.

36
00:03:05.133 --> 00:03:08.943
The core grew modestly too, and gene counts cannot prove wiring.

37
00:03:10.383 --> 00:03:16.123
Even so, animal signaling expanded by copying old pathways, mostly at the receptor

38
00:03:16.123 --> 00:03:16.543
end.
